<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://schema.phpunit.de/10.5/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="tests/Bootstrap.php"
         cacheDirectory=".phpunit.cache"
         colors="true"
         executionOrder="depends,defects"
         beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests="false"
         failOnRisky="false"
         failOnWarning="false"
>
    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="warp">
            <directory>tests</directory>
            <exclude>tests/Bootstrap.php</exclude>
            <exclude>tests/Pest.php</exclude>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>
    <source>
        <include>
            <directory suffix=".php">src</directory>
        </include>
    </source>
    <php>
        <ini name="error_reporting" value="-1"/>
        <ini name="display_errors" value="stderr"/>

        <!--
            DB isolation. DDEV exports CRAFT_DB_DATABASE=db (the playground's
            live schema) into the container shell, and craft-pest-core's
            InstallsCraft plugin boots Craft from a fresh vendor/bin/pest
            process before PHPUnit itself ever applies these env entries.
            InstallsCraft works around that ordering problem in
            loadPhpunitXmlEnvironmentVariables(), which reads getcwd() slash
            phpunit.xml or phpunit.xml.dist directly and pins $_SERVER,
            $_ENV, and putenv() before Craft boots, but only when getcwd() is
            this file's own directory. Run the suite from Warp's own root
            (vendor/bin/pest, or composer test), never via the shared
            playground's console pest command with a configuration flag
            pointing at this file, which leaves getcwd() at the playground
            root and silently defeats this override so Craft boots against
            the live db instead. tests/Bootstrap.php asks the connection
            which database it landed on and aborts the run when the answer is
            not the name below, so that failure is loud rather than silent.

            warp_test is Warp's own test database, deliberately not the
            estate-shared db_test. A shared database is also a shared
            project-config mutex namespace, so two plugin suites running at
            the same time deadlock each other with BusyResourceException,
            "1213 Deadlock found" and "1412 Table definition has changed"
            long before either one asserts anything. Provision it once:

                ddev exec -s db -u root mysql -uroot -proot -e
                  "CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS warp_test
                   CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci;
                   GRANT ALL ON warp_test.* TO 'db'@'%';"

            Only CRAFT_DB_DATABASE and CRAFT_DB_TABLE_PREFIX are forced here:
            those are the only two values DDEV's ambient environment gets
            wrong for a suite that needs its own schema (DDEV already exports
            the correct driver/server/port/user/password, and warp_test lives
            on the same MySQL instance as the playground's live db).
            Deliberately NOT forcing driver/server/port/user/password keeps
            this file portable to CI, which provisions its own MySQL service
            on its own host/credentials via ordinary (non-forced) job env.

            Keep this value in step with WARP_TEST_DATABASE in
            tests/Bootstrap.php and MYSQL_DATABASE in
            .github/workflows/tests.yaml.
        -->
        <env name="CRAFT_DB_DATABASE" value="warp_test" force="true"/>
        <env name="CRAFT_DB_TABLE_PREFIX" value="" force="true"/>

        <!--
            Mutex isolation, and the other half of running two suites at once.
            Craft's project-config lock is a MySQL GET_LOCK (see
            craft\helpers\App::dbMutexConfig), and GET_LOCK names are scoped to
            the MySQL *server*, not to a database. The only thing separating
            one install's locks from another's is the key prefix,
            Craft::$app->getEnvId(). Every plugin suite in the estate otherwise
            boots under the same env id and takes the same server-wide lock, so
            a private database on its own does not help: the second suite to
            reach project config still dies with BusyResourceException.

            It has to be CRAFT_ENVIRONMENT, not CRAFT_APP_ID. Core reads
            CRAFT_ENVIRONMENT in bootstrap/bootstrap.php, whereas its own
            src/config/app.php hard-codes 'id' => 'CraftCMS'; the
            App::env('CRAFT_APP_ID') line belongs to the craftcms/craft starter
            project's config/app.php, which a plugin repo does not have. Setting
            CRAFT_APP_ID here is inert, and was, until this was corrected.

            This also outranks craft-pest-core, whose bootstrap defines
            CRAFT_ENVIRONMENT as 'production' for every suite in the estate:
            InstallsCraft::loadPhpunitXmlEnvironmentVariables() pins $_SERVER
            first, and App::env() checks $_SERVER ahead of defined constants.

            getEnvId() reaches nothing else that outlives the process: the
            mutex key prefix, the session state key prefix, and the web-user
            state key prefix are its only readers in core.
        -->
        <env name="CRAFT_ENVIRONMENT" value="warp-test" force="true"/>
    </php>
</phpunit>
